-- title:
--   Theorem 14, first assertion — acceptance probability at most $\inf_{\lambda>0}e^{-\lambda\tau}\Lambda_{\mathcal Q}(\lambda)^N$ for any profile (the remaining clauses are the goal)
-- statement:
--   Consider a routed run with credits $u_A,u_B>0$, $N$ rounds and a threshold $\tau\in\mathbb R$, and let $\mathcal Q\subseteq[0,1]^2$ be a soundness profile of the run. Then the verifier accepts with probability at most
--
--   $$
--   \mathbb P[S\ge\tau]\le\inf_{\lambda>0}e^{-\lambda\tau}\Lambda_{\mathcal Q}(\lambda)^N .
--   $$
--
--   This is the first assertion of Theorem 14; the exact supremum on $\mathcal Q_\gamma$, the bound under (HA) and the gating clause are the other clauses of the goal `SigRoute.theorem_14`.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: every prover interacting with the verifier on a false statement → every probability space $(\Omega,\mu)$, every filtration $(\mathcal F_j)$, every family of post-base-stage σ-algebras $\mathcal G_j$ and every adapted run $(\rho,a)$ (`IsRoutedRun`); reason: the run of any prover is such a run, with $\mathcal F_j$ generated by the first $j$ rounds and $\mathcal G_j$ by $\mathcal F_{j-1}$ and $\nu_j$; the theorem specializes to the paper's
--   - encoding: the hypothesis $x\notin L$ → not modelled; reason: the run is the run on a false statement; the hypotheses (H0), (HA) and the forgery bound are what $x\notin L$ supplies
--   - encoding: rounds $j=1,\dots,N$ conditioned on $\mathcal F_{j-1}$, answers $a_j\in\{0,1\}$ → 0-based rounds $j<N$ conditioned on `F j`, answers in `Bool`; reason: relabelling; same instances
--   - encoding: $\mathbb P[S\ge\tau]$ and conditional probabilities $\mathbb P[E\mid\mathcal F]$ almost surely → `μ.real {ω | τ ≤ creditSum …}` and `condProb` (Mathlib `condExp` of the indicator), with `∀ᵐ`; reason: Lean convention; the events are measurable, so these are the paper's quantities
--   - encoding: $\inf_{\lambda>0}$ and $\sup_{(q_A,q_B)\in\mathcal Q}$ → real `⨅` over `Set.Ioi 0` and real `sSup` of the image of $\mathcal Q$; reason: the family is nonnegative and $\mathcal Q\subseteq[0,1]^2$ is bounded, so both are the true infimum and supremum
-- source:
--   Dumlu, Signal-Routed Two-Arm Verification, STOC 2027 submission manuscript (held version, sha256 d56b3b7a…), Theorem 14, first and second sentences, p.7

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SigRoute_IsProfile

namespace SigRoute

open MeasureTheory

/-- Theorem 14, first assertion (arbitrary profile) (Dumlu, Signal-Routed Two-Arm Verification, STOC 2027 submission manuscript (held version, sha256 d56b3b7a…), Theorem 14, first two sentences, p.7): if
`Q` is a soundness profile of the run, then the verifier accepts (`S ≥ τ`) with probability at most
`inf_{λ>0} e^{−λτ} Λ_Q(λ)^N`. The remaining clauses of Theorem 14 are the goal `theorem_14`.

**Formalization Note** The paper's "every prover" is "every probability space `(Ω, μ)`, filtration `F`, post-base-stage σ-algebras `G` and adapted run `(ρ, a)`" (`IsRoutedRun`); rounds are 0-based, so the paper's `F_{j−1}` is `F j`. The infimum is the real `⨅` over `λ ∈ (0, ∞)`; the family is
nonnegative, so it is the true infimum. Credits are any `u_A, u_B > 0` and `τ` any real. -/
theorem theorem_14_profile_bound {Ω : Type*} {m : MeasurableSpace Ω} (μ : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (F : Filtration ℕ m) (G : ℕ → MeasurableSpace Ω)
    (ρ : ℕ → Ω → Arm) (a : ℕ → Ω → Bool) (hrun : IsRoutedRun F G ρ a)
    (u : Arm → ℝ) (hu : ∀ X, 0 < u X) (N : ℕ) (τ : ℝ) (Q : Set (ℝ × ℝ))
    (hQ : IsProfile μ F ρ a N Q) :
    μ.real {ω | τ ≤ creditSum u ρ a N ω}
      ≤ ⨅ lam : Set.Ioi (0 : ℝ), Real.exp (-(lam : ℝ) * τ) * Lam u Q lam ^ N := by sorry

end SigRoute
